.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
.Dd October 11, 2026
.Dt DTFL_STREAM_BRANCH 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm dtfl_stream_branch ,
.Nm dtfl_stream_branch_when
.Nd split a stream into two by a predicate
.Sh LIBRARY
Timely and differential dataflow library
.Pq libdtfl , \-ldtfl
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In dtfl/timely_ops.h
.Ft void
.Fo dtfl_stream_branch
.Fa "dtfl_stream *st"
.Fa "dtfl_tpred_fn pred"
.Fa "dtfl_ctx ctx"
.Fa "dtfl_stream **yes"
.Fa "dtfl_stream **no"
.Fc
.Ft void
.Fo dtfl_stream_branch_when
.Fa "dtfl_stream *st"
.Fa "bool (*pred)(void *ctx, const uint64_t *time)"
.Fa "dtfl_ctx ctx"
.Fa "dtfl_stream **yes"
.Fa "dtfl_stream **no"
.Fc
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn dtfl_stream_branch
splits
.Fa st
into two streams by a per-record predicate over the record and its
time: records for which
.Fa pred
returns
.Dv true
go to
.Pf * Fa yes ,
the rest to
.Pf * Fa no .
.Pp
.Fn dtfl_stream_branch_when
splits whole messages by a predicate over the time alone: when
.Fa pred
returns
.Dv true
for a message's time the whole message goes to
.Pf * Fa yes ,
otherwise to
.Pf * Fa no .
Use it instead of
.Fn dtfl_stream_branch
when the decision depends only on the time.
.Pp
Both outputs carry the input's record type.
Neither operator needs the input frontier.
The context
.Fa ctx
is released by its destructor when the operator is dropped (PLAN D5).
.Sh RETURN VALUES
Both functions store the two output streams through
.Fa yes
and
.Fa no .
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
dtfl_stream *evens, *odds;
dtfl_stream_branch(s, pred_even, DTFL_NOCTX, &evens, &odds);
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr dtfl_stream_ok_err 3 ,
.Xr dtfl_stream_partition 3 ,
.Xr dtfl 7
.Sh HISTORY
These are the C form of timely's
.Vt Branch
and
.Vt BranchWhen
traits.
They are public at the pinned release
.Pq Li timely-v0.31.0
and were removed upstream afterward (#818); the port keeps the pinned
behavior.
.Sh THREAD SAFETY
The streams and the operator belong to the thread that runs the worker
and must not be used from another thread (PLAN section 5).
